Liana-Roll

Ingredients: LIQUID FUNK + HIGHLIFE + ETHEREAL, DREAMY & AIRY + Fiddle + 2020s ERA  
Creative Catalyst: Founding artists were field botanists who sonified the micro-voltage fluctuations of living rainforest foliage.

A buoyant, weightless subgenre where interlocking Ghanaian palm-wine guitar riffs and microtonally sliding fiddle melodies soar over rolling 172-BPM liquid drum & bass breaks, threaded with vaporous synthesizer pads mapped directly from leaf galvanic data.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 170–174 BPM. An aerodynamic, sub-heavy rolling two-step drum pattern juxtaposed against West African highlife’s syncopated 12/8 bell patterns, played out on brushed ghost snares and muted hand-percussion.
- Harmonic Palette: Sweet, buoyant major-key Highlife progressions (I–IV–I–V and ii–V–I) voiced in glassy suspended 9ths and 11ths, juxtaposed with the mournful, microtonal slides and airy harmonic overtones of rustic fiddle playing.
- Production Signatures: 
  1. Bio-Voltage Sidechaining: Galvanic micro-voltages logged from live ficus and mahogany leaves are routed into sidechain compressors, making lush master-bus reverbs breathe organically with the forest’s biorhythm.
  2. Chamber-Air Bowing: Acoustic fiddle tracks are double-mic'd with vintage ribbons, passed through multi-tap stereo delays, and high-passed above 400 Hz to float like mist over the mix.
  3. Palm-Clean Guitar Layering: Dual single-coil electric guitars fingerpicked with thumb and index finger, panned hard left and right, dry as bone to leave pristine acoustic space for deep, rounded 808 sub-bass glides.
- Dynamics: Songs open as tranquil, ambient forest recordings and meandering solo fiddle laments before dropping cleanly into effortless, high-velocity roller basslines; tracks expand by budding and blooming new melodic counter-lines rather than relying on abrasive EDM builds.

Scene & Origin Lore
The sound materialized between 2022 and 2024 at the Bobiri Butterfly Sanctuary in Ashanti, Ghana, where visiting sound artists from Bristol set up biodata synthesis gear alongside resident highlife guitarists and visiting Scottish acoustic researchers. What began as an ecological research installation tracking water-stress electrical spikes in old-growth timber rapidly mutated into late-night forest jam sessions. Within months, underground listening clubs spread through Kumasi and Accra, quickly mirrored in rainy UK loft parties. The scene’s devotees—known informally as "sap-tappers"—are recognized by chlorophyll-dyed linen smocks, antique brass leaf brooches, and galvanic monitor clips clipped like badges onto their lapels. Their signature gathering is the "dew-shift": all-night open-air dances in humid botanical gardens that peak exactly as the morning sun cuts through canopy fog.

Typical Instruments: Piezoelectric-pickup acoustic fiddle, thumb-picked Fender Telecaster/Stratocaster, Eurorack bio-data CV interfaces (PlantWave / MIDI Sprout), Roland TR-808 sub-bass, gankogui bells, shekere, vintage upright piano, granular ambient pads.

For Fans Of: Ebo Taylor, LTJ Bukem, Erland Cooper, Calibre.

