Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Sundog Swallowed the Calyx
- Story & Context: During a freak electrical storm over the Bobiri canopy research towers, sound engineer Marcus Adebayo watched a blinding atmospheric sundog flare across the sky just as a lightning surge overloaded their timber-clamp telemetry arrays, baking an erratic, syncopated 172-BPM voltage cycle straight into the sampler. Kirstin grabbed her fiddle to match the shrieking galvanic pitch while Kwadwo laughed, fingerpicking sweet palm-wine scales beneath the chaos until the fried equipment settled into an ecstatic, breathy liquid rhythm. The trio finished tracking before the rain even cleared, shouting through the tent as the tape machine hissed with the opening lyric hook, "The leaf has a current the thunder cannot drown."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Extreme macro art photography of a glistening tropical mahogany leaf fitted with tiny gold electrode pins, rain droplets glowing like prisms, with a luminous parhelion sundog halo blazing softly through the misty forest canopy overhead, emerald green and incandescent gold palette.

Track 2: Asuo Virga
- Story & Context: Stationed on a solo field survey along the Pra River, assistant researcher Ama Serwaa watched summer rain evaporate into warm updrafts miles before ever touching the scorched soil, the air smelling intensely of parched dust and dry riverbed silt. Paralyzed by homesickness and isolation, she taped galvanic sensors to a dying river willow and sobbed into the team’s field recorder, allowing the plant’s desperate electrical drops to pitch-shift Kirstin's recorded fiddle into soaring, ghostly lamentations. She secretly submitted the stem to the band's Bristol Dropbox as a demo, unaware her broken spoken confession would form the track's iconic soaring hook: "Count every volt before the morning takes my name."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric cinematic rendering of tropical virga rain curtains drifting and evaporating mid-sky over a sunlit Ghanaian river valley, soft turquoise and burnt amber tones, ethereal weather haze, 16mm film grain aesthetic.

Track 3: "Will the Halo Hold Until Morning?"
- Story & Context: Long after the researchers dismantled their field tent at Kakum National Park and returned to Bristol, local guides discovered that wrapping discarded copper wire around the roots of the central silk-cotton tree caused battery-powered transistor radios to catch stray, echoing liquid-funk frequencies out of thin air. Villagers gathered under a lunar halo on the first dry-season night, dancing barefoot on mossy loam to ghostly fiddle glissandos and clean guitar arpeggios that hummed straight out of the wet bark. The phenomenon vanished at sunrise, leaving only a lingering communal chorus etched into village lore: "We left the wire in the wood to watch the sky catch fire."
